{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwsiHsH6vzz9geoUyea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KHvDCe6Uvmdc1kNVjALvXh","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050232__xd92716/work/DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232/src/58-about-text.js","content":"/* 58-about-text: the About & Credits pages' words, and the one source of truth for the exit interview\n * (tools/exit-interview.mjs writes ../exit-interview.json from DH.ABOUT). Plain text. */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst DH = window.DH;\nDH.ABOUT = {\n  sourceInformation: {\n    date: '2026-11-04 05:02:32',\n    source: 'Densha de Go! (Taito, 1996 arcade and its home versions): the Japanese train-driving game of stopping on the mark and keeping the timetable to the second',\n    plan: 'PLAN.md',\n    genre: 'Train driving simulation with arcade scoring',\n    perspective: 'First-person cab view (pseudo-3D rail renderer), with chase, high chase, platform and trackside cameras, a side-view Stop Cam and a top-down ring map for stage select',\n    tech: 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) with a new rail.js pseudo-3D railway renderer forked from its ROAD module; AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol 1); vanilla JavaScript, no other libraries or CDN; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests and tools',\n    artDirection: 'The engine\\'s PS1-era pixel style (320x240 minimum, expanded to the screen, E.tones hue-shifted shading, stepped alpha); new code-drawn trains (Halo 8000, Halo 3000, Sky Liner, freight), cab dashboards, 4-lamp signals, line-side boards, stations, a parametric Japanese townscape and hand-drawn landmarks; time-of-day colour grades, rain, fog and glare; engine Humanoid and Blob rigs for the cast and crowds',\n    animation: 'The engine\\'s Humanoid and Blob rigs (idle, gait, wave, cheer) plus new bow, point and flag poses; code-tweened cab controls (the master controller, needles, lamps, wipers, sun visor), the white-gloved point-and-call hand, doors, crossing gates, the Ferris wheel and lighthouse beam, Haro-kun\\'s squash and spinning halo, the rank stamp slam and postcard flip',\n    audio: 'All procedural Web Audio in code: a continuous train synth (VVVF inverter singing, resistor growl, rolling, wind, tunnel echo, bridge roar, rain, brake squeal, compressor, horn), rail-joint clicks, door chimes, the conductor\\'s buzzer, ATS bell, crossing bells with Doppler, 12 original station departure melodies, 10 chip-music songs on the engine sequencer, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices for every character (Babble syllable synthesis with formants)',\n    online: 'AI Hub Session Protocol 1 in ghost mode for 2-4 players (the Parallel Run: each player on their own track, the others shown as ghost trains, live standings, the game\\'s own card); local 2-player split-screen Duel; no other network use',\n    testing: 'One command (node test/run.mjs) prints only failures and a count: headless suites over the renderer math, route compiler, physics check values, signals and ATS, limits, stations and judgments, calls and horn, timetable calibration, scoring and ranks, events, input and glyphs, sound parameters, Babble, story, autopilot, attract, Dev Mode, progress, a mock AI Hub, the duel, settings and screens, plus a scripted playthrough of the whole game; Playwright browser checks of the bundle (title, attract, keyboard and mouse driving, touch-only) and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; host leaving); screenshots for look review',\n    research: 'Read the engine handoff docs (README, API card, road module and kit, tests) and the AI Hub protocol README and examples; no web research (Densha de Go! mechanics from general knowledge)',\n    environment: 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown',\n    model: 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh',\n    usage: 'unknown'\n  },\n  testingFeedback: [\n    'How many issues did testing catch? About 40 that I fixed before shipping. The biggest groups: the arrival-time estimator in the autopilot (always late, then always early, until it modelled the final creep), signal lookups that walked the loop in route order instead of distance order (a missed YY into the terminal), a scripted freight that never stopped (its stop check sat below its braking floor), ground bands drawn over the ballast, a viaduct with no deck, comfort draining to zero from the autopilot\\'s lever moves, and assorted menu, scene and bundle issues.',\n    'How many would be considered critical or game breaking? About 8: the S2 freight that blocked the line forever (S2 could not be finished), the timetables (every stage ended in a game over for a good driver before calibration), the missing YY speed check at the terminal, the viaduct pit, and a few crashes on scene changes and in the bundle.',\n    'What was the most helpful test you ran? Driving every stage headless with the autopilot and printing each event with the clock: it found the freight deadlock, the signal-ordering bug, the timetable problems and the scoring bugs in seconds, and it became the full scripted playthrough.',\n    'What was the least helpful test you ran? Pixel-level screenshot checks of menus at several sizes: they took the longest and mostly confirmed layouts I could have reasoned about from the code.'\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'What did you like most about the engine? Its headless determinism: game.advance with exact fixed steps and input.tap/hold made a 30-minute career testable in seconds, and the Humanoid rigs, Shell settings and chip audio meant a whole cast, menus and sound needed no assets.',\n    'Did you get stuck or guess wrong? The ROAD module projects y with half the height, which squashes the world on wide screens, and it has no multiple tracks, raised strips or rear cameras, so I forked it into a new renderer. I guessed the rig view yaw for figures in the rail view and the Dialog\\'s voice timing before reading the code.',\n    'What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? The rail renderer (square-pixel pseudo-3D with tracks, strips with faces and patterns, things, crest clipping, rear views, fog and time-of-day tinting), and on top of it the train dynamics and the model-predictive autopilot.',\n    'What was the hardest part about using the engine? Finding the right internals for things the API card does not cover (the Shell\\'s settings rows, the audio buses, the pose solver) in a 14,700-line file; the API index helped, but several changes needed reading the source.',\n    'What did you change in the engine, and why? A Dialog voice hook (per-character gibberish), a settings-screen group filter (Dev Mode sliders, hidden from standard Settings), PlayStation glyphs by default plus face-button and menu glyphs in the font, audio bus accessors (the continuous train synth follows volume and ducking), bow/point/flag poses (staff, workers, the finale), and the hub\\'s Create+Options rule in the pad reader; plus a new rail.js module. All are logged in ENGINE-CHANGES.md.',\n    'If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be? Ship a general pseudo-3D strip renderer (or make ROAD square-pixel with lateral objects and rear views), since vehicle games beyond OutRun need platforms, trains and buildings beside the line.'\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'What did you like most about this prompt? It is specific about the things that matter for a real product (10-foot controller-first UI, glyph switching, attract mode, a dev mode with live sliders, the hub, one test command) and leaves the creative design open.',\n    'What was confusing? Whether Source Information should describe the plan\\'s intentions or what was actually built, and how the plan and TASK.md title rules interact (STAGE.md says to use the plan\\'s title, TASK.md says to pick one by RNG; the plan had already done that).',\n    'What was the hardest thing to do? Making a long, faithful career (six stages of real timetables) play well end to end and staying deterministic for tests, while also covering attract mode, local and online multiplayer, and all the front-end screens.',\n    'What would you change in this prompt? Say how long the demo should take to play, and whether a scripted full playthrough must pass on the bundle or on the dev page; and allow a shorter required career when a plan is very large.'\n  ],\n  aiHub: {\n    name: 'Densha de Halo!',\n    tagline: 'Stop on the mark. Leave on the second.',\n    description: 'Drive the Halo Line, a loop railway round Akari City, from the first train at dawn to the last train at night: stop exactly on the mark, keep the timetable to the second, and point and call every signal. Race up to three friends on the four-track Parallel Run.',\n    type: 'Game',\n    genre: 'Simulation',\n    source: 'Densha de Go!',\n    series: 'Densha de Go!',\n    players: '1–4',\n    controls: 'Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△ · EB Backspace/L1',\n    tags: ['first-person', 'vehicles', 'arcade', 'retro', 'versus', 'story']\n  }\n};\nconst SI = [['Date', 'date'], ['Source', 'source'], ['Plan', 'plan'], ['Genre', 'genre'], ['Perspective', 'perspective'], ['Tech', 'tech'], ['Art Direction', 'artDirection'], ['Animation', 'animation'], ['Audio', 'audio'], ['Online', 'online'], ['Testing', 'testing'], ['Research', 'research'], ['Environment', 'environment'], ['Model', 'model'], ['Usage', 'usage']];\nDH.SOURCE_LABELS = SI.map(q => q[0]);\nDH.ABOUT_PAGES = () => {\n  const A = DH.ABOUT, q = (list) => list.map(t => { const i = t.indexOf('? '); return i > 0 ? [['h', t.slice(0, i + 1)], ['p', t.slice(i + 2)]] : [['p', t]]; }).flat();\n  return [\n    { tab: 'CREDITS', body: [['h', 'DENSHA DE HALO!'], ['p', 'An original homage to Densha de Go! (Taito). Not affiliated with or endorsed by Taito. The Halo Line, Akari City and everyone in them are made up for this game.'], ['h', 'MADE BY'], ['p', 'Anthropic Claude (claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh), building from PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort).'], ['h', 'ENGINE'], ['p', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 by Michael Crosato (MIT License), the full engine from its handoff package, with a new rail renderer forked from its ROAD module.'], ['h', 'ONLINE'], ['p', 'AI Hub Session Protocol 1, client session.js 1.4.0, pasted unmodified.'], ['h', 'ASSETS'], ['p', 'None from files: every picture, sound, song and voice is made in code.'], ['h', 'THANKS'], ['p', 'To every driver who ever pointed at a signal and said it out loud.']] },\n    { tab: 'SOURCE INFO', body: SI.map(([l, k]) => [['h', l.toUpperCase()], ['p', A.sourceInformation[k]]]).flat() },\n    { tab: 'TESTING', body: q(A.testingFeedback) },\n    { tab: 'ENGINE', body: q(A.engineFeedback) },\n    { tab: 'PROMPT', body: q(A.promptFeedback) }\n  ];\n};\n})();\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1932,"cache_read_input_tokens":660134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1932},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b1bf799-1946-4a15-a1b7-75e4a7d99d2e","uuid":"727a45f8-791a-423d-9799-c045fd7285aa","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:14:01.980Z","request_id":"req_011CghwshvYDAfKmMuii3nmh","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KHvDCe6Uvmdc1kNVjALvXh":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050232__xd92716/work/DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232/src/58-about-text.js","content":"/* 58-about-text: the About & Credits pages' words, and the one source of truth for the exit interview\n * (tools/exit-interview.mjs writes ../exit-interview.json from DH.ABOUT). Plain text. */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst DH = window.DH;\nDH.ABOUT = {\n  sourceInformation: {\n    date: '2026-11-04 05:02:32',\n    source: 'Densha de Go! (Taito, 1996 arcade and its home versions): the Japanese train-driving game of stopping on the mark and keeping the timetable to the second',\n    plan: 'PLAN.md',\n    genre: 'Train driving simulation with arcade scoring',\n    perspective: 'First-person cab view (pseudo-3D rail renderer), with chase, high chase, platform and trackside cameras, a side-view Stop Cam and a top-down ring map for stage select',\n    tech: 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) with a new rail.js pseudo-3D railway renderer forked from its ROAD module; AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol 1); vanilla JavaScript, no other libraries or CDN; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests and tools',\n    artDirection: 'The engine\\'s PS1-era pixel style (320x240 minimum, expanded to the screen, E.tones hue-shifted shading, stepped alpha); new code-drawn trains (Halo 8000, Halo 3000, Sky Liner, freight), cab dashboards, 4-lamp signals, line-side boards, stations, a parametric Japanese townscape and hand-drawn landmarks; time-of-day colour grades, rain, fog and glare; engine Humanoid and Blob rigs for the cast and crowds',\n    animation: 'The engine\\'s Humanoid and Blob rigs (idle, gait, wave, cheer) plus new bow, point and flag poses; code-tweened cab controls (the master controller, needles, lamps, wipers, sun visor), the white-gloved point-and-call hand, doors, crossing gates, the Ferris wheel and lighthouse beam, Haro-kun\\'s squash and spinning halo, the rank stamp slam and postcard flip',\n    audio: 'All procedural Web Audio in code: a continuous train synth (VVVF inverter singing, resistor growl, rolling, wind, tunnel echo, bridge roar, rain, brake squeal, compressor, horn), rail-joint clicks, door chimes, the conductor\\'s buzzer, ATS bell, crossing bells with Doppler, 12 original station departure melodies, 10 chip-music songs on the engine sequencer, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices for every character (Babble syllable synthesis with formants)',\n    online: 'AI Hub Session Protocol 1 in ghost mode for 2-4 players (the Parallel Run: each player on their own track, the others shown as ghost trains, live standings, the game\\'s own card); local 2-player split-screen Duel; no other network use',\n    testing: 'One command (node test/run.mjs) prints only failures and a count: headless suites over the renderer math, route compiler, physics check values, signals and ATS, limits, stations and judgments, calls and horn, timetable calibration, scoring and ranks, events, input and glyphs, sound parameters, Babble, story, autopilot, attract, Dev Mode, progress, a mock AI Hub, the duel, settings and screens, plus a scripted playthrough of the whole game; Playwright browser checks of the bundle (title, attract, keyboard and mouse driving, touch-only) and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; host leaving); screenshots for look review',\n    research: 'Read the engine handoff docs (README, API card, road module and kit, tests) and the AI Hub protocol README and examples; no web research (Densha de Go! mechanics from general knowledge)',\n    environment: 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown',\n    model: 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh',\n    usage: 'unknown'\n  },\n  testingFeedback: [\n    'How many issues did testing catch? About 40 that I fixed before shipping. The biggest groups: the arrival-time estimator in the autopilot (always late, then always early, until it modelled the final creep), signal lookups that walked the loop in route order instead of distance order (a missed YY into the terminal), a scripted freight that never stopped (its stop check sat below its braking floor), ground bands drawn over the ballast, a viaduct with no deck, comfort draining to zero from the autopilot\\'s lever moves, and assorted menu, scene and bundle issues.',\n    'How many would be considered critical or game breaking? About 8: the S2 freight that blocked the line forever (S2 could not be finished), the timetables (every stage ended in a game over for a good driver before calibration), the missing YY speed check at the terminal, the viaduct pit, and a few crashes on scene changes and in the bundle.',\n    'What was the most helpful test you ran? Driving every stage headless with the autopilot and printing each event with the clock: it found the freight deadlock, the signal-ordering bug, the timetable problems and the scoring bugs in seconds, and it became the full scripted playthrough.',\n    'What was the least helpful test you ran? Pixel-level screenshot checks of menus at several sizes: they took the longest and mostly confirmed layouts I could have reasoned about from the code.'\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'What did you like most about the engine? Its headless determinism: game.advance with exact fixed steps and input.tap/hold made a 30-minute career testable in seconds, and the Humanoid rigs, Shell settings and chip audio meant a whole cast, menus and sound needed no assets.',\n    'Did you get stuck or guess wrong? The ROAD module projects y with half the height, which squashes the world on wide screens, and it has no multiple tracks, raised strips or rear cameras, so I forked it into a new renderer. I guessed the rig view yaw for figures in the rail view and the Dialog\\'s voice timing before reading the code.',\n    'What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? The rail renderer (square-pixel pseudo-3D with tracks, strips with faces and patterns, things, crest clipping, rear views, fog and time-of-day tinting), and on top of it the train dynamics and the model-predictive autopilot.',\n    'What was the hardest part about using the engine? Finding the right internals for things the API card does not cover (the Shell\\'s settings rows, the audio buses, the pose solver) in a 14,700-line file; the API index helped, but several changes needed reading the source.',\n    'What did you change in the engine, and why? A Dialog voice hook (per-character gibberish), a settings-screen group filter (Dev Mode sliders, hidden from standard Settings), PlayStation glyphs by default plus face-button and menu glyphs in the font, audio bus accessors (the continuous train synth follows volume and ducking), bow/point/flag poses (staff, workers, the finale), and the hub\\'s Create+Options rule in the pad reader; plus a new rail.js module. All are logged in ENGINE-CHANGES.md.',\n    'If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be? Ship a general pseudo-3D strip renderer (or make ROAD square-pixel with lateral objects and rear views), since vehicle games beyond OutRun need platforms, trains and buildings beside the line.'\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'What did you like most about this prompt? It is specific about the things that matter for a real product (10-foot controller-first UI, glyph switching, attract mode, a dev mode with live sliders, the hub, one test command) and leaves the creative design open.',\n    'What was confusing? Whether Source Information should describe the plan\\'s intentions or what was actually built, and how the plan and TASK.md title rules interact (STAGE.md says to use the plan\\'s title, TASK.md says to pick one by RNG; the plan had already done that).',\n    'What was the hardest thing to do? Making a long, faithful career (six stages of real timetables) play well end to end and staying deterministic for tests, while also covering attract mode, local and online multiplayer, and all the front-end screens.',\n    'What would you change in this prompt? Say how long the demo should take to play, and whether a scripted full playthrough must pass on the bundle or on the dev page; and allow a shorter required career when a plan is very large.'\n  ],\n  aiHub: {\n    name: 'Densha de Halo!',\n    tagline: 'Stop on the mark. Leave on the second.',\n    description: 'Drive the Halo Line, a loop railway round Akari City, from the first train at dawn to the last train at night: stop exactly on the mark, keep the timetable to the second, and point and call every signal. Race up to three friends on the four-track Parallel Run.',\n    type: 'Game',\n    genre: 'Simulation',\n    source: 'Densha de Go!',\n    series: 'Densha de Go!',\n    players: '1–4',\n    controls: 'Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△ · EB Backspace/L1',\n    tags: ['first-person', 'vehicles', 'arcade', 'retro', 'versus', 'story']\n  }\n};\nconst SI = [['Date', 'date'], ['Source', 'source'], ['Plan', 'plan'], ['Genre', 'genre'], ['Perspective', 'perspective'], ['Tech', 'tech'], ['Art Direction', 'artDirection'], ['Animation', 'animation'], ['Audio', 'audio'], ['Online', 'online'], ['Testing', 'testing'], ['Research', 'research'], ['Environment', 'environment'], ['Model', 'model'], ['Usage', 'usage']];\nDH.SOURCE_LABELS = SI.map(q => q[0]);\nDH.ABOUT_PAGES = () => {\n  const A = DH.ABOUT, q = (list) => list.map(t => { const i = t.indexOf('? '); return i > 0 ? [['h', t.slice(0, i + 1)], ['p', t.slice(i + 2)]] : [['p', t]]; }).flat();\n  return [\n    { tab: 'CREDITS', body: [['h', 'DENSHA DE HALO!'], ['p', 'An original homage to Densha de Go! (Taito). Not affiliated with or endorsed by Taito. The Halo Line, Akari City and everyone in them are made up for this game.'], ['h', 'MADE BY'], ['p', 'Anthropic Claude (claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh), building from PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort).'], ['h', 'ENGINE'], ['p', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 by Michael Crosato (MIT License), the full engine from its handoff package, with a new rail renderer forked from its ROAD module.'], ['h', 'ONLINE'], ['p', 'AI Hub Session Protocol 1, client session.js 1.4.0, pasted unmodified.'], ['h', 'ASSETS'], ['p', 'None from files: every picture, sound, song and voice is made in code.'], ['h', 'THANKS'], ['p', 'To every driver who ever pointed at a signal and said it out loud.']] },\n    { tab: 'SOURCE INFO', body: SI.map(([l, k]) => [['h', l.toUpperCase()], ['p', A.sourceInformation[k]]]).flat() },\n    { tab: 'TESTING', body: q(A.testingFeedback) },\n    { tab: 'ENGINE', body: q(A.engineFeedback) },\n    { tab: 'PROMPT', body: q(A.promptFeedback) }\n  ];\n};\n})();\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KHvDCe6Uvmdc1kNVjALvXh","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050232__xd92716/work/DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232/src/58-about-text.js (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b1bf799-1946-4a15-a1b7-75e4a7d99d2e","uuid":"e35c6c07-d342-4366-8bd6-deb6816ea9c8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:14:02.002Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050232__xd92716/work/DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232/src/58-about-text.js","content":"/* 58-about-text: the About & Credits pages' words, and the one source of truth for the exit interview\n * (tools/exit-interview.mjs writes ../exit-interview.json from DH.ABOUT). Plain text. */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst DH = window.DH;\nDH.ABOUT = {\n  sourceInformation: {\n    date: '2026-11-04 05:02:32',\n    source: 'Densha de Go! (Taito, 1996 arcade and its home versions): the Japanese train-driving game of stopping on the mark and keeping the timetable to the second',\n    plan: 'PLAN.md',\n    genre: 'Train driving simulation with arcade scoring',\n    perspective: 'First-person cab view (pseudo-3D rail renderer), with chase, high chase, platform and trackside cameras, a side-view Stop Cam and a top-down ring map for stage select',\n    tech: 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) with a new rail.js pseudo-3D railway renderer forked from its ROAD module; AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol 1); vanilla JavaScript, no other libraries or CDN; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests and tools',\n    artDirection: 'The engine\\'s PS1-era pixel style (320x240 minimum, expanded to the screen, E.tones hue-shifted shading, stepped alpha); new code-drawn trains (Halo 8000, Halo 3000, Sky Liner, freight), cab dashboards, 4-lamp signals, line-side boards, stations, a parametric Japanese townscape and hand-drawn landmarks; time-of-day colour grades, rain, fog and glare; engine Humanoid and Blob rigs for the cast and crowds',\n    animation: 'The engine\\'s Humanoid and Blob rigs (idle, gait, wave, cheer) plus new bow, point and flag poses; code-tweened cab controls (the master controller, needles, lamps, wipers, sun visor), the white-gloved point-and-call hand, doors, crossing gates, the Ferris wheel and lighthouse beam, Haro-kun\\'s squash and spinning halo, the rank stamp slam and postcard flip',\n    audio: 'All procedural Web Audio in code: a continuous train synth (VVVF inverter singing, resistor growl, rolling, wind, tunnel echo, bridge roar, rain, brake squeal, compressor, horn), rail-joint clicks, door chimes, the conductor\\'s buzzer, ATS bell, crossing bells with Doppler, 12 original station departure melodies, 10 chip-music songs on the engine sequencer, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices for every character (Babble syllable synthesis with formants)',\n    online: 'AI Hub Session Protocol 1 in ghost mode for 2-4 players (the Parallel Run: each player on their own track, the others shown as ghost trains, live standings, the game\\'s own card); local 2-player split-screen Duel; no other network use',\n    testing: 'One command (node test/run.mjs) prints only failures and a count: headless suites over the renderer math, route compiler, physics check values, signals and ATS, limits, stations and judgments, calls and horn, timetable calibration, scoring and ranks, events, input and glyphs, sound parameters, Babble, story, autopilot, attract, Dev Mode, progress, a mock AI Hub, the duel, settings and screens, plus a scripted playthrough of the whole game; Playwright browser checks of the bundle (title, attract, keyboard and mouse driving, touch-only) and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; host leaving); screenshots for look review',\n    research: 'Read the engine handoff docs (README, API card, road module and kit, tests) and the AI Hub protocol README and examples; no web research (Densha de Go! mechanics from general knowledge)',\n    environment: 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown',\n    model: 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh',\n    usage: 'unknown'\n  },\n  testingFeedback: [\n    'How many issues did testing catch? About 40 that I fixed before shipping. The biggest groups: the arrival-time estimator in the autopilot (always late, then always early, until it modelled the final creep), signal lookups that walked the loop in route order instead of distance order (a missed YY into the terminal), a scripted freight that never stopped (its stop check sat below its braking floor), ground bands drawn over the ballast, a viaduct with no deck, comfort draining to zero from the autopilot\\'s lever moves, and assorted menu, scene and bundle issues.',\n    'How many would be considered critical or game breaking? About 8: the S2 freight that blocked the line forever (S2 could not be finished), the timetables (every stage ended in a game over for a good driver before calibration), the missing YY speed check at the terminal, the viaduct pit, and a few crashes on scene changes and in the bundle.',\n    'What was the most helpful test you ran? Driving every stage headless with the autopilot and printing each event with the clock: it found the freight deadlock, the signal-ordering bug, the timetable problems and the scoring bugs in seconds, and it became the full scripted playthrough.',\n    'What was the least helpful test you ran? Pixel-level screenshot checks of menus at several sizes: they took the longest and mostly confirmed layouts I could have reasoned about from the code.'\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'What did you like most about the engine? Its headless determinism: game.advance with exact fixed steps and input.tap/hold made a 30-minute career testable in seconds, and the Humanoid rigs, Shell settings and chip audio meant a whole cast, menus and sound needed no assets.',\n    'Did you get stuck or guess wrong? The ROAD module projects y with half the height, which squashes the world on wide screens, and it has no multiple tracks, raised strips or rear cameras, so I forked it into a new renderer. I guessed the rig view yaw for figures in the rail view and the Dialog\\'s voice timing before reading the code.',\n    'What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? The rail renderer (square-pixel pseudo-3D with tracks, strips with faces and patterns, things, crest clipping, rear views, fog and time-of-day tinting), and on top of it the train dynamics and the model-predictive autopilot.',\n    'What was the hardest part about using the engine? Finding the right internals for things the API card does not cover (the Shell\\'s settings rows, the audio buses, the pose solver) in a 14,700-line file; the API index helped, but several changes needed reading the source.',\n    'What did you change in the engine, and why? A Dialog voice hook (per-character gibberish), a settings-screen group filter (Dev Mode sliders, hidden from standard Settings), PlayStation glyphs by default plus face-button and menu glyphs in the font, audio bus accessors (the continuous train synth follows volume and ducking), bow/point/flag poses (staff, workers, the finale), and the hub\\'s Create+Options rule in the pad reader; plus a new rail.js module. All are logged in ENGINE-CHANGES.md.',\n    'If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be? Ship a general pseudo-3D strip renderer (or make ROAD square-pixel with lateral objects and rear views), since vehicle games beyond OutRun need platforms, trains and buildings beside the line.'\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'What did you like most about this prompt? It is specific about the things that matter for a real product (10-foot controller-first UI, glyph switching, attract mode, a dev mode with live sliders, the hub, one test command) and leaves the creative design open.',\n    'What was confusing? Whether Source Information should describe the plan\\'s intentions or what was actually built, and how the plan and TASK.md title rules interact (STAGE.md says to use the plan\\'s title, TASK.md says to pick one by RNG; the plan had already done that).',\n    'What was the hardest thing to do? Making a long, faithful career (six stages of real timetables) play well end to end and staying deterministic for tests, while also covering attract mode, local and online multiplayer, and all the front-end screens.',\n    'What would you change in this prompt? Say how long the demo should take to play, and whether a scripted full playthrough must pass on the bundle or on the dev page; and allow a shorter required career when a plan is very large.'\n  ],\n  aiHub: {\n    name: 'Densha de Halo!',\n    tagline: 'Stop on the mark. Leave on the second.',\n    description: 'Drive the Halo Line, a loop railway round Akari City, from the first train at dawn to the last train at night: stop exactly on the mark, keep the timetable to the second, and point and call every signal. Race up to three friends on the four-track Parallel Run.',\n    type: 'Game',\n    genre: 'Simulation',\n    source: 'Densha de Go!',\n    series: 'Densha de Go!',\n    players: '1–4',\n    controls: 'Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△ · EB Backspace/L1',\n    tags: ['first-person', 'vehicles', 'arcade', 'retro', 'versus', 'story']\n  }\n};\nconst SI = [['Date', 'date'], ['Source', 'source'], ['Plan', 'plan'], ['Genre', 'genre'], ['Perspective', 'perspective'], ['Tech', 'tech'], ['Art Direction', 'artDirection'], ['Animation', 'animation'], ['Audio', 'audio'], ['Online', 'online'], ['Testing', 'testing'], ['Research', 'research'], ['Environment', 'environment'], ['Model', 'model'], ['Usage', 'usage']];\nDH.SOURCE_LABELS = SI.map(q => q[0]);\nDH.ABOUT_PAGES = () => {\n  const A = DH.ABOUT, q = (list) => list.map(t => { const i = t.indexOf('? '); return i > 0 ? [['h', t.slice(0, i + 1)], ['p', t.slice(i + 2)]] : [['p', t]]; }).flat();\n  return [\n    { tab: 'CREDITS', body: [['h', 'DENSHA DE HALO!'], ['p', 'An original homage to Densha de Go! (Taito). Not affiliated with or endorsed by Taito. The Halo Line, Akari City and everyone in them are made up for this game.'], ['h', 'MADE BY'], ['p', 'Anthropic Claude (claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh), building from PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort).'], ['h', 'ENGINE'], ['p', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 by Michael Crosato (MIT License), the full engine from its handoff package, with a new rail renderer forked from its ROAD module.'], ['h', 'ONLINE'], ['p', 'AI Hub Session Protocol 1, client session.js 1.4.0, pasted unmodified.'], ['h', 'ASSETS'], ['p', 'None from files: every picture, sound, song and voice is made in code.'], ['h', 'THANKS'], ['p', 'To every driver who ever pointed at a signal and said it out loud.']] },\n    { tab: 'SOURCE INFO', body: SI.map(([l, k]) => [['h', l.toUpperCase()], ['p', A.sourceInformation[k]]]).flat() },\n    { tab: 'TESTING', body: q(A.testingFeedback) },\n    { tab: 'ENGINE', body: q(A.engineFeedback) },\n    { tab: 'PROMPT', body: q(A.promptFeedback) }\n  ];\n};\n})();\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KHvDCe6Uvmdc1kNVjALvXh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
