undle.mjs && node tools/exit-interview.mjs && grep -c \"07:43\" ../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232.html ../exit-interview.json","description":"Correct the session time; rebuild bundle and exit interview"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kkjyw3KAKCXfniFPt8vJ9Y","type":"tool_result","content":"1\nwrote DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232.html (1680 KB, 34 scripts)\nwrote exit-interview.json (parses, every field filled)\n../exit-interview.json:1\n../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232.html:1\n[This command modified 1 file you've previously read: src/58-about-text.js. Call Read before editing.]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b1bf799-1946-4a15-a1b7-75e4a7d99d2e","uuid":"25e38ba7-68f7-4bcc-8691-06506da01c16","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:42:44.872Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1\nwrote DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232.html (1680 KB, 34 scripts)\nwrote exit-interview.json (parses, every field filled)\n../exit-interview.json:1\n../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232.html:1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"staleReadFileStateHint":"[This command modified 1 file you've previously read: src/58-about-text.js. Call Read before editing.]"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Kkjyw3KAKCXfniFPt8vJ9Y","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi4hYP7AH3oZMmGLVNiP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **Densha de Halo!**, the title the plan picked by RNG. It's a train-driving game in the spirit of Densha de Go!: you drive a 20.6 km loop railway round a made-up city, stop exactly on the mark, keep the timetable to the second, and point and call every signal. Your instructor is retiring today, and the career runs from the dawn depot to his last train at midnight.\n\nThe final check passed: 281 checks, 0 failures — 264 headless including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, 14 Playwright browser checks and 3 AI Hub harness runs, all against the final file. After that run I changed two small things: the hub's round-length tag (5 → 6 minutes, since a round takes just over 5) and the session time in the About text. I re-ran only the hub harness after the tag change (3 of 3 passed).\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Career:** a tutorial (S0) and five service stages (S1–S5) with real timetables. A good driver can arrive on the second; the autopilot ranks S on every stage with every arrival on time.\n- **The rules:** a notched lever with brake lag, judged stops (within 10 cm is a PERFECT HALO), smooth stops, block signals and ATS, speed limits, horn boards, point-and-call. Halo Time is your life. There is also a passenger comfort rating.\n- **Story events:** a freight that blocks you in the rain, a festival obstruction on a crossing, track workers, a late salaryman holding the doors, a rival express at sunset, and fog and a headlight-flash farewell on the last train.\n- **Look:** a new pseudo-3D rail renderer on top of the engine's pixel style, with stations, crowds, townscapes, landmarks, time of day and weather.\n- **Sound:** all made in code — the train's inverter \"singing\", 12 station departure melodies, 10 songs, and gibberish voices for every character.\n- **Everything else:** Free Run, a split-screen Duel, a 2–4 player AI Hub ghost race, attract mode after 10 s idle, Dev Mode with 19 live sliders and Reset, a Driver's Log with eight postcards, How to Drive, and About & Credits.\n\n**Two things worth knowing**\n- The plan's own timetables were too tight: a good driver ran out of Halo Time. A calibration tool now sets every arrival 6–12 s after the autopilot's fastest run.\n- Frame rate in headless Chromium is 39–60 FPS; the dense city at sunset is the slowest at about 29 ms a frame. Performance was out of scope, so I left it there.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:32\n- **Source:** Densha de Go! (Taito, 1996 arcade and home versions)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Train driving simulation with arcade scoring. The main view is the first-person cab (pseudo-3D rail renderer), with chase, high chase, platform and trackside cameras, a side-view Stop Cam, and a top-down ring map for stage select.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) plus a new `rail.js` renderer forked from its ROAD module. AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol 1). Plain JavaScript, no other libraries or CDN. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests and tools.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's PS1-era pixel style. New code-drawn trains, cabs, 4-lamp signals, line-side boards, stations, a parametric Japanese townscape and landmarks. Time-of-day grades, rain, fog and glare. Engine Humanoid and Blob rigs for the cast and crowds.\n- **Animation:** Engine rigs plus new bow, point and flag poses. Code-tweened cab controls, the white-gloved point-and-call hand, doors, crossing gates, wipers, the sun visor, the Ferris wheel and lighthouse. Haro-kun's bounce and spinning halo, the rank stamp slam, the postcard flip.\n- **Audio:** All procedural Web Audio:\n  - a continuous train synth (inverter singing or resistor growl, rolling, wind, tunnel echo, bridge, rain, brake squeal, compressor, horn);\n  - rail-joint clicks, door chimes, the conductor's buzzer, the ATS bell, crossing bells with Doppler;\n  - 12 departure melodies and 10 chip songs;\n  - gibberish voices built from syllables and formants.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol 1, ghost mode, 2–4 players (the Parallel Run, with the game's own card). Local 2-player split-screen Duel. No other network use.\n- **Testing:** Headless scripts (game.advance, input taps, game.debug), a scripted playthrough of the whole game, Playwright browser checks, the AI Hub harness, and screenshots for look review. One command, `node test/run.mjs`, runs 27 suites (264 checks) in parallel and prints only failures and a count. `--browser --hub --bundle` adds 14 browser checks and 3 harness runs on the final file.\n- **Research:** Read the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol and examples. No web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** API cost unknown; tokens unknown; session time about 2 hours 40 minutes (05:02 to about 07:43)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 40, all fixed:\n  - 23 from the headless suites and autopilot runs, e.g. a freight that never stopped, a train that couldn't start on P1, a timing estimate that was first always late and then always early, signal lookups that went wrong past the loop's seam, the HUD running off small screens;\n  - 15 from screenshots, e.g. ground drawn over the track, a viaduct with no deck, a tunnel that painted the whole sky, people and lamp glows that were never drawn;\n  - 1 from the browser checks (a menu click only picked the highlighted row);\n  - plus 3 mistakes in the tests themselves.\n- **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** 8:\n  - the S2 freight deadlock (S2 couldn't be finished);\n  - the P1 start (the tutorial's first step was impossible);\n  - the plan's timetables (good drivers ran out of Halo Time);\n  - an obstruction that never cleared (S3 got stuck);\n  - two crashes;\n  - a missed speed check on the terminal's restricted signal;\n  - people and lamp glows never drawn.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** Driving every stage headless with the autopilot and printing each event with the clock. It found most of the rule bugs in seconds and grew into the full playthrough.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Three early checks that only asserted `true` (I made them real later), and the first serial run of the suite, which took over 15 minutes until I split it across worker processes.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Headless determinism: exact fixed steps, input taps and game.debug made a 30-minute career testable in seconds. The rigs, Shell menus and chip synth meant a whole cast, every menu and all the sound needed no assets.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?**\n  - The ROAD module squashes wide screens and has no multiple tracks, raised platforms or rear cameras, so I forked it.\n  - I guessed the camera angle for drawing rigs in my own view.\n  - I assumed the engine's Menu could scale its text; it can't, so I wrote a bigger one.\n  - I didn't expect E.store to lose saves when localStorage is missing (headless runs, blocked storage), so I added an in-memory fallback.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The rail renderer, then the train physics and the predictive autopilot. The autopilot drives attract mode, the tests and the timetable calibration.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Finding internals the API card doesn't cover (settings rows, audio buses, the pose solver, Dialog typing) in a 14,700-line file.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n  - A Dialog voice hook, for per-character gibberish.\n  - A group filter on the Settings screen, which gives Dev Mode's live sliders with Reset and keeps them out of the normal Settings.\n  - PlayStation glyphs by default, plus ✕ ○ □ △ in the font.\n  - Audio bus accessors, so the train synth follows volume and ducking.\n  - Bow, point and flag poses, for platform staff, track workers and the finale.\n  - The hub's Create+Options rule in the gamepad reader.\n  - The new `rail.js` module.\n\n  All are logged in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Ship a general pseudo-3D strip renderer (or make ROAD square-pixel with roadside objects and rear views).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** It is specific where a real product needs it (10-foot controller-first UI, glyph switching, attract mode, Dev Mode sliders, the hub, one test command) and leaves the design open.\n- **What was confusing?** Whether Source Information should describe what the plan intended or what was actually built, and how the plan's title interacts with TASK.md's RNG rule (the plan had already rolled it).\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Making six stages with real timetables play well end to end and stay deterministic for tests, alongside attract mode, local and online multiplayer, and every front-end screen.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** State the intended play length, whether the scripted playthrough must pass on the bundle or the dev page, and allow a shorter career when a plan is very large.\n\nFiles are in the work folder:\n- DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232.html\n- exit-interview.json\n- DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050232/ (dev folder; README.md there lists the commands)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":939,"cache_read_input_tokens":941369,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":939},"output_tokens":720,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b1bf799-1946-4a15-a1b7-75e4a7d99d2e","uuid":"890c4841-c0e2-4a3a-99b5-29f3d214efeb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:43:14.363Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi4hXyHjLE4NgNu1e4pA"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The final reply contains all four required sections (Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback), and exit-interview.json was written and validated as parsing with every field filled. The bundled HTML exists in the folder root, and the transcript shows a final run of 281 passing checks on it. However, the requirement that every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs is not clearly established. Several TASK.md items have only partial or no evidence: the Xbox glyph option was checked only in a headless settings test, the Duel's ordering of keyboard halves was not verified in a browser, and the Dev Mode sound board and the 'Trigger an event' tool were never exercised end to end. The transcript also shows the assistant changing the Dev Mode and About text late in the run and rebuilding the bundle after the last full run, so the final bundle's coverage of the full suite is not fully shown. Nothing in the transcript shows an explicit TODO or stub left in the code, but the completeness of every feature is not demonstrated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b1bf799-1946-4a15-a1b7-75e4a7d99d2e","uuid":"043ab614-6a62-41e4-8053-3b82aeb4fab0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:43:20.181Z","isSynthetic":true}
